<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Yamato</h1><p class="st-meta">250 CE – 701 CE (approx.) · capital Asuka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The early Japanese kingship of the Nara basin that built giant keyhole tombs, adopted Buddhism and laid the foundations of the Japanese imperial state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From the mid-3rd century huge keyhole-shaped burial mounds appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> basin, marking a confederation of chiefs led by a great king, or okimi. By the 5th century Yamato rulers, recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as the five kings of Wa, controlled much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>; an inscribed sword from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inariyama-sword">Inariyama</a> names King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuryaku">Wakatakeru</a>, usually identified as Yuryaku.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Power rested on hereditary clans (uji) ranked by titles and bound to the court, with specialist groups of craftsmen, many of Korean origin. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> arrived from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/baekje">Baekje</a> in the 6th century and was championed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soga-clan">Soga clan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-shotoku">Prince Shotoku</a> issued a seventeen-article code in 604, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taika-reform">Taika reforms</a> of 645 began a centralised land and tax system on Chinese lines.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-baekgang">Baekgang</a> in 663 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshin-war">Jinshin war</a> of 672 led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-tenmu">Tenmu</a> and his successors to build a bureaucratic state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiho-code">Taiho code</a> of 701 completed the transition to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ritsuryo-japan">Ritsuryo Japan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yamato.jpg" alt="Yamato" width="320" height="294"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamato_Kingship_in_East_Asia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>250 CE – 701 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Asuka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yuryaku, Suiko, Tenmu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shinto, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yamato kingship, Yamato polity, Wa, Yamato court</dd></dl></aside></div>
